{"crc":"78232ce3","kind":"Candidate","payload":{"response":"Looking at the issue, the problem is that `str()` on the `pytest.raises` context variable doesn't behave the same as a normal exception catch. When you do `str(e)` on a caught exception, you get the full error message, but when you do `str(e)` on the `pytest.raises` context variable, you get a truncated representation.\n\nThe issue is in the `ExceptionInfo.__repr__` method in `src/_pytest/_code/code.py`. Currently, it returns a short representation, but it should return the full exception string representation to match the behavior of normal exception handling.\n\nLet me look at the relevant code:\n\n```python\ndef __repr__(self):\n    if self._excinfo is None:\n        return \"<ExceptionInfo for raises contextmanager>\"\n    return \"<ExceptionInfo %s tblen=%d>\" % (self.typename, len(self.traceback))\n```\n\nThis should be changed to return the full exception string when the exception info is available. The `__str__` method is inherited from `__repr__` (since `__str__` is not defined), so both `str()` and `repr()` return this short form.\n\nThe fix should make `__repr__` return the full exception representation (using `exconly()` or similar) when the exception info is available.\n\nLooking at the `exconly` method:\n```python\ndef exconly(self, tryshort=False):\n    lines = format_exception_only(self.type, self.value)\n    text = \"\".join(lines)\n    text = text.rstrip()\n    if tryshort:\n        if text.startswith(self._striptext):\n            text = text[len(self._striptext):]\n    return text\n```\n\nThe fix should update `__repr__` to return the full exception string when available:\n\n# path: src/_pytest/_code/code.py\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\n    def __repr__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return \"<ExceptionInfo for raises contextmanager>\"\n        return \"<ExceptionInfo %s tblen=%d>\" % (self.typename, len(self.traceback))\n=======\n    def __repr__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return \"<ExceptionInfo for raises contextmanager>\"\n        return \"<ExceptionInfo %s tblen=%d>\" % (self.typename, len(self.traceback))\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return \"<ExceptionInfo for raises contextmanager>\"\n        return self.exconly()\n>>>>>>> REPLACE","round":1,"slot":0},"producer":{"instance":"01KZT1GZYKHWFGNZ24F62341KW","kind":"drafter","parent":"01KZT1GZYDT58BZX1SN3CQWHVG"},"schema":"Candidate@1","seq":14,"t":1786506678.230927}
{"crc":"13bcf47e","kind":"substrate.TriggerFired","payload":{"factory":"validator","firing_key":null,"input_sha256":"sha256:37d51a9e2fee87aef1b590707fbdfa8c4bd5e722255d62d9f2ceacbabe26c4f7","instance":"01KZT1H3YQNDZZDE11TVWKEDCA","resolved_input":{"response":"Looking at the issue, the problem is that `str()` on the `pytest.raises` context variable doesn't behave the same as a normal exception catch. When you do `str(e)` on a caught exception, you get the full error message, but when you do `str(e)` on the `pytest.raises` context variable, you get a truncated representation.\n\nThe issue is in the `ExceptionInfo.__repr__` method in `src/_pytest/_code/code.py`. Currently, it returns a short representation, but it should return the full exception string representation to match the behavior of normal exception handling.\n\nLet me look at the relevant code:\n\n```python\ndef __repr__(self):\n    if self._excinfo is None:\n        return \"<ExceptionInfo for raises contextmanager>\"\n    return \"<ExceptionInfo %s tblen=%d>\" % (self.typename, len(self.traceback))\n```\n\nThis should be changed to return the full exception string when the exception info is available. The `__str__` method is inherited from `__repr__` (since `__str__` is not defined), so both `str()` and `repr()` return this short form.\n\nThe fix should make `__repr__` return the full exception representation (using `exconly()` or similar) when the exception info is available.\n\nLooking at the `exconly` method:\n```python\ndef exconly(self, tryshort=False):\n    lines = format_exception_only(self.type, self.value)\n    text = \"\".join(lines)\n    text = text.rstrip()\n    if tryshort:\n        if text.startswith(self._striptext):\n            text = text[len(self._striptext):]\n    return text\n```\n\nThe fix should update `__repr__` to return the full exception string when available:\n\n# path: src/_pytest/_code/code.py\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\n    def __repr__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return \"<ExceptionInfo for raises contextmanager>\"\n        return \"<ExceptionInfo %s tblen=%d>\" % (self.typename, len(self.traceback))\n=======\n    def __repr__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return \"<ExceptionInfo for raises contextmanager>\"\n        return \"<ExceptionInfo %s tblen=%d>\" % (self.typename, len(self.traceback))\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return \"<ExceptionInfo for raises contextmanager>\"\n        return self.exconly()\n>>>>>>> REPLACE","round":1,"slot":0},"trigger_id":"validate"},"producer":null,"schema":"substrate.TriggerFired@1","seq":15,"t":1786506678.231081}
